From: Oliver Meyer <olm@cativa.net>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] uboot_env: use the other copy when one redundant copy cannot
 be read completely

libuboot_load() returned -EIO as soon as one copy of a redundant
environment could not be read completely, even when the other copy was
valid. fw_printenv/fw_setenv then fell back to the default environment
file, and the next store wrote that default (plus the change) as the
newest copy: the valid environment was replaced. On a FAT file a short
copy is what a power cut during U-Boot's saveenv can leave behind.

Treat such a copy as invalid (like a bad CRC), as U-Boot's
env_check_redund() does: the valid copy is used and the next store
rewrites the bad one. A non-redundant environment still fails with
-EIO.

Signed-off-by: Oliver Meyer <olm@cativa.net>
Upstream: not submitted (Hi-Fi Bridge, docs/OTA.md section 4.1)
---
 src/uboot_env.c | 17 +++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/uboot_env.c b/src/uboot_env.c
--- a/src/uboot_env.c
+++ b/src/uboot_env.c
@@ -645,8 +645,21 @@
 		dev = &ctx->envdevs[i];
 		ret = devread(ctx, i, buf[i]);
 		if (ret != ctx->size) {
-			free(buf[0]);
-			return -EIO;
+			/*
+			 * A copy that cannot be read completely (file missing or
+			 * shorter than the environment, e.g. after a power cut
+			 * while it was rewritten) is an invalid copy. With a
+			 * redundant environment the other copy is still used,
+			 * as U-Boot does (env_check_redund), and the next store
+			 * rewrites the bad one.
+			 */
+			if (!ctx->redundant) {
+				free(buf[0]);
+				return -EIO;
+			}
+			crcenv[i] = false;
+			dev->flags = 0;
+			continue;
 		}
 		crc = *(uint32_t *)(buf[i] + offsetcrc);
 		dev->crc = crc32(0, (uint8_t *)data, usable_envsize);
